{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "טרף", :translit "taraf", :gloss "torn off / plucked"} {:he "ב' במסורה", :translit "bet be-mesorah", :gloss "two in the Masorah"} {:he "הכא", :translit "hakha", :gloss "here"} {:he "כי הוא טרף וירפאנו", :translit "ki hu taraf va-yirpa'enu", :gloss "for he tore it and he healed us"} {:he "מה התם לשון שבירה", :translit "mah hetam leshon shevirah", :gloss "what there is an expression of breaking"} {:he "אף הכא נמי", :translit "af hakha nami", :gloss "so here too"} {:he "שברתו מן האילן", :translit "shebarto min ha-ilan", :gloss "she broke it from the tree"} {:he "צף על המים", :translit "tzaf al ha-mayim", :gloss "floating on the water"} {:he "קלו המים", :translit "kalu ha-mayim", :gloss "the waters diminished"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["טרף" "כי הוא טרף וירפאנו"]}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim cites a Masoretic count: the word taraf appears twice in Scripture — here in Genesis 8:11 and in Genesis 31:39. He then links the two via a gezerah shavah (verbal analogy), since both use the lemma taraf. In Genesis 31:39 Jacob tells Laban that torn-apart animals he never brought home, establishing taraf as an expression of violent breaking or tearing. Applying that meaning to the dove's leaf, he reads it as torn from the tree, which signaled to Noah that the waters had receded.", :sources ["Genesis 8:11" "Genesis 31:39"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["טרף" "כי הוא טרף וירפאנו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "טרף", :refs ["Genesis 8:11" "Genesis 37:33" "Genesis 44:28" "Exodus 22:12" "Numbers 23:24" "Isaiah 5:29" "Ezekiel 19:3" "Ezekiel 19:6"], :n 24, :lens :exact} {:phrase "כי הוא טרף וירפאנו", :refs ["Hosea 6:1"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:04:58.918477340Z", :model "glm-5.2"}